(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-4-renal-physiology-i-quiz"]={"title":"Renal Physiology I — Quiz (65 Questions)","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"The detrusor muscle is composed primarily of:","choices":["Cardiac muscle","Elastic connective tissue","Smooth muscle","Skeletal muscle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the detrusor is smooth muscle arranged in interlacing bundles, contracting involuntarily under parasympathetic (pelvic nerve) control to empty the bladder. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The primary function of the detrusor muscle is to:","choices":["Produce urine","Relax the bladder during filling","Close the urethra","Contract the bladder during urination"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: detrusor contraction raises bladder pressure and generates the force that expels urine during micturition — its defining function. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The trigone is located:","choices":["Within the ureter","Inside the renal pelvis","At the dome of the bladder","Between the ureter openings and urethra"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the trigone is the smooth-walled triangular region bounded by the two ureteral orifices and the internal urethral opening. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which sphincter is under voluntary control?","choices":["Detrusor muscle","Internal urethral sphincter","Internal anal sphincter","External urethral sphincter"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the external urethral sphincter is skeletal muscle innervated by the somatic pudendal nerve, giving voluntary control over voiding. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Parasympathetic fibers supplying the bladder travel in the:","choices":["Hypogastric nerve","Pudendal nerve","Vagus nerve","Pelvic nerve"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: parasympathetic fibers from S2–S4 travel in the pelvic nerve to the bladder, driving detrusor contraction during voiding. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The pelvic nerve originates from:","choices":["S2-S4","C5-C8","L1-L2","T5-T9"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the pelvic nerve arises from the sacral parasympathetic outflow at S2–S4. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Sympathetic stimulation of the bladder primarily promotes:","choices":["Urine storage","Bladder emptying","External sphincter relaxation","Detrusor contraction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: sympathetic (hypogastric) tone relaxes the detrusor and contracts the internal sphincter, favoring urine storage. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which nerve controls the external urethral sphincter?","choices":["Vagus","Pudendal","Pelvic","Hypogastric"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the somatic pudendal nerve provides voluntary control of the skeletal-muscle external urethral sphincter. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The hypogastric nerve primarily:","choices":["Contracts the detrusor","Relaxes the detrusor","Contracts skeletal muscle","Detects stretch"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: sympathetic input via the hypogastric nerve relaxes the detrusor and closes the internal sphincter, favoring storage. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Loss of pudendal nerve function would MOST directly impair:","choices":["Glomerular filtration","Kidney blood flow","Voluntary control of urination","RAAS activation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the pudendal nerve provides voluntary control of the external urethral sphincter, so losing it most directly impairs voluntary continence and voiding control. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Bladder stretch receptors send sensory information primarily through the:","choices":["Pelvic nerve","Pudendal nerve","Phrenic nerve","Hypogastric nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: afferent stretch signals from the bladder wall travel centrally via the pelvic nerve, triggering the micturition reflex. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which pairing is CORRECT?","choices":["Pudendal = voluntary control","Hypogastric = urination","Pelvic = storage","Vagus = bladder contraction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the pudendal nerve gives voluntary control over the external urethral sphincter. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The micturition reflex begins when:","choices":["Blood pressure rises","Stretch receptors in the bladder wall are activated","ADH is released","Plasma osmolarity falls"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: as the bladder fills, stretch receptors in its wall are activated, and their afferent signals initiate the micturition reflex. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The micturition reflex is primarily a:","choices":["Cortical reflex","Brainstem reflex","Autonomic spinal reflex","Somatic reflex"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the micturition reflex is fundamentally an autonomic spinal cord reflex centered on the sacral segments (S2–S4), modulated by higher centers. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The spinal cord segments involved are:","choices":["C5-C8","S2-S4","T1-T4","L1-L2"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the micturition reflex is centered on the sacral spinal segments S2–S4. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"During normal urination, the detrusor muscle:","choices":["Does not change","Relaxes","Contracts only voluntarily","Contracts"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: during urination, the detrusor contracts, generating the pressure needed to expel urine. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The internal urethral sphincter:","choices":["Is skeletal muscle","Is controlled by the pudendal nerve","Relaxes during urination","Contracts during urination"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the smooth-muscle internal urethral sphincter relaxes during voiding, permitting urine outflow. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The external urethral sphincter is relaxed by:","choices":["Pelvic nerves","RAAS activation","Hypogastric stimulation","Pudendal reflex inhibition"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: voluntary voiding involves withdrawing (inhibiting) pudendal somatic drive, which relaxes the external urethral sphincter. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which brain region normally inhibits the micturition reflex until an appropriate time?","choices":["Medulla only","Cerebral cortex","Cerebellum","Hypothalamus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the cerebral cortex exerts voluntary inhibitory control over the sacral reflex, holding voiding in check until it is socially appropriate. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Normally, after complete urination, approximately how much urine remains?","choices":["100 mL","5-10 mL","250 mL","0 mL"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: after normal, complete voiding, only a small residual volume (roughly 5–10 mL) remains in the bladder. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Increasing abdominal pressure voluntarily helps initiate urination by:","choices":["Activating sympathetic nerves","Stimulating stretch receptors","Closing the urethra","Lowering bladder pressure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: a Valsalva-type abdominal squeeze raises bladder pressure and further stretches the bladder wall receptors, reinforcing the micturition reflex. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which event occurs FIRST?","choices":["Stretch receptors activate","Detrusor contracts","External sphincter relaxes","Urine exits urethra"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: stretch receptor activation as the bladder fills is the initiating event that triggers the rest of the micturition reflex. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Failure of voluntary relaxation of the external sphincter would MOST likely result in:","choices":["Proteinuria","Polyuria","Urinary retention","Increased GFR"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: if the external sphincter cannot relax voluntarily, outflow is obstructed and urine is retained in the bladder. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The micturition reflex becomes stronger as:","choices":["Blood pressure rises","Plasma glucose rises","Bladder filling increases","Plasma sodium rises"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: as bladder filling increases, stretch-receptor firing increases proportionally, progressively strengthening the micturition reflex. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Glomerular filtration is:","choices":["Reabsorption into blood","Secretion into tubules","Excretion of urine","Filtration of plasma into Bowman's capsule"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: glomerular filtration is the bulk movement of a protein-free plasma ultrafiltrate from the glomerular capillaries into Bowman's capsule. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Normal GFR is closest to:","choices":["125 mL/min","75 mL/min","25 mL/min","500 mL/min"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: normal GFR is approximately 125 mL/min. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Approximately how much filtrate is produced each day?","choices":["20 L","180 L","500 L","2 L"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: about 125 mL/min sustained over 1440 minutes per day yields roughly 180 L of filtrate daily, over 99% of which is reabsorbed. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which substance should normally NOT appear in glomerular filtrate?","choices":["Sodium","Albumin","Glucose","Water"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: albumin is large and carries a net negative charge, so it is normally excluded by the size- and charge-selective filtration barrier. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The glomerular filtration barrier consists of all EXCEPT:","choices":["Mesangial cells","Basement membrane","Fenestrated endothelium","Podocytes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: mesangial cells provide structural support and contractile function within the glomerular tuft but are not one of the three layers of the filtration barrier. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Podocytes primarily function to:","choices":["Reabsorb glucose","Form filtration slits","Produce renin","Produce erythropoietin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: podocyte foot processes interdigitate to form filtration slits (bridged by slit diaphragms), the final size barrier of the glomerulus. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Albumin is minimally filtered because it is:","choices":["Negatively charged","Lipid soluble","Too small","Positively charged"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: beyond its size, albumin's negative charge is repelled by the negatively charged glomerular basement membrane, further limiting its filtration. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Mesangial cells:","choices":["Provide structural support and contractile function","Are part of the filtration barrier","Form filtration slits","Produce urine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: mesangial cells provide structural support for the glomerular capillary tuft and can contract to modulate the surface area available for filtration. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which equation correctly describes GFR?","choices":["GFR = PG − PB","GFR = Kf × Net Filtration Pressure","GFR = BP × HR","GFR = RBF × FF"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: GFR = Kf × NFP, where Kf is the filtration coefficient (permeability × surface area) and NFP is the net filtration pressure. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Kf represents:","choices":["Permeability × Surface Area","Blood flow","Pressure gradient","Oncotic pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Kf (the filtration coefficient) equals capillary permeability multiplied by the available surface area for filtration. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which substance is considered the gold standard for measuring GFR?","choices":["Inulin","Glucose","Creatinine","Albumin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: inulin is freely filtered and neither reabsorbed nor secreted by the tubules, so its clearance exactly equals GFR, making it the gold standard. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Creatinine clearance slightly:","choices":["Underestimates GFR","Has no relation to GFR","Equals GFR exactly","Overestimates GFR"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: a small amount of creatinine is secreted by the renal tubules in addition to being filtered, so creatinine clearance slightly overestimates true GFR. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Filtration fraction equals:","choices":["RPF/GFR","RBF/GFR","GFR/RBF","GFR/RPF"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: filtration fraction equals GFR divided by renal plasma flow (RPF), normally about 0.2. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Normal filtration fraction is approximately:","choices":["20%","80%","50%","5%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: normally about 20% of renal plasma flow is filtered at the glomerulus. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The clearance equation is:","choices":["U/P","VP/U","PU/V","UV/P"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: clearance equals (urine concentration × urine flow rate) divided by plasma concentration, i.e., UV/P. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which pressure PROMOTES filtration?","choices":["Bowman's capsule hydrostatic pressure","Capsular oncotic pressure","Glomerular hydrostatic pressure","Plasma oncotic pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure (PG) is the dominant force driving fluid out of the capillary and into Bowman's space, promoting filtration. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which pressure OPPOSES filtration?","choices":["PB","Kf","PG","πB"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Bowman's capsule hydrostatic pressure (PB) pushes back against outward fluid movement, opposing filtration, along with plasma oncotic pressure. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Bowman's capsule oncotic pressure is normally:","choices":["Equal to plasma oncotic pressure","Moderate","Approximately zero","High"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: because filtrate is normally protein-free, Bowman's capsule (capsular) oncotic pressure is approximately zero. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Increasing glomerular hydrostatic pressure causes:","choices":["Lower GFR","Higher GFR","No change","Lower RPF only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: raising glomerular hydrostatic pressure (PG) increases net filtration pressure, which increases GFR. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Increasing Bowman's capsule hydrostatic pressure causes:","choices":["Increased FF","Increased GFR","Increased RPF","Decreased GFR"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: higher PB opposes filtration, reducing net filtration pressure and thus GFR (as seen in urinary tract obstruction). — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Increasing plasma protein concentration will:","choices":["Decrease GFR","Not affect GFR","Increase GFR","Increase filtration fraction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: increased plasma protein concentration raises plasma oncotic pressure, which opposes filtration and reduces GFR. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Constriction of the afferent arteriole causes:","choices":["Decreased GFR","Increased GFR","Increased PG","Increased FF"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: afferent arteriolar constriction reduces blood flow into the glomerulus, lowering glomerular hydrostatic pressure (PG) and therefore GFR. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Mild efferent constriction:","choices":["Eliminates filtration","Lowers PG","Usually increases GFR","Decreases FF"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: mild efferent arteriolar constriction increases resistance to outflow, raising glomerular hydrostatic pressure (PG) and thus increasing GFR. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Severe efferent constriction eventually:","choices":["Has no effect","Decreases GFR because oncotic pressure rises","Raises GFR indefinitely","Eliminates plasma proteins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: with severe efferent constriction, blood moves through the glomerulus so slowly that plasma oncotic pressure rises progressively along the capillary, opposing filtration and lowering GFR. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which determinant of GFR is under the greatest physiological control?","choices":["PB","Kf","πB","PG"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: glomerular hydrostatic pressure (PG) is tightly regulated via afferent and efferent arteriolar tone, making it the most actively controlled determinant of GFR. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The myogenic mechanism responds primarily to:","choices":["ADH","Plasma potassium","Increased arterial pressure","Plasma sodium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: when arterial pressure rises, stretch of the afferent arteriolar smooth muscle triggers reflex contraction (the myogenic response), helping stabilize renal blood flow and GFR. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The macula densa senses:","choices":["Sodium chloride","Glucose","Oxygen","Potassium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: macula densa cells in the early distal tubule sense NaCl delivery, the signal underlying tubuloglomerular feedback. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"High NaCl delivery to the macula densa causes:","choices":["Renin release","Efferent dilation","Increased GFR","Afferent constriction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: high NaCl delivery triggers ATP/adenosine release from the macula densa, constricting the afferent arteriole to lower GFR back toward normal. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Low NaCl at the macula densa stimulates:","choices":["ATP release","ANP release","Renin release","ADH release"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: low NaCl delivery to the macula densa signals juxtaglomerular cells to release renin, activating RAAS to help restore renal perfusion. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The juxtaglomerular apparatus consists primarily of:","choices":["Macula densa and juxtaglomerular cells","Collecting duct and loop of Henle","Podocytes and mesangial cells","Bowman's capsule and glomerulus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the juxtaglomerular apparatus consists of the macula densa (in the early distal tubule) and the juxtaglomerular (granular, renin-secreting) cells of the afferent arteriole. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"RAAS primarily acts to:","choices":["Decrease blood pressure","Lower GFR","Maintain GFR during low perfusion","Increase urine production"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: RAAS defends renal perfusion and GFR during states of low blood flow/pressure, through angiotensin II-mediated vasoconstriction (including preferential efferent constriction) and aldosterone-driven volume retention. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"ANP generally:","choices":["Decreases filtration","Increases sympathetic tone","Increases GFR","Releases renin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: ANP dilates the afferent arteriole and constricts the efferent arteriole, raising GFR and promoting natriuresis. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Sympathetic stimulation of the kidney generally:","choices":["Eliminates autoregulation","Has no effect","Raises GFR","Lowers GFR"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: strong renal sympathetic activation constricts afferent arterioles, reducing renal blood flow and GFR. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Renal prostaglandins primarily:","choices":["Increase albumin filtration","Block filtration","Protect renal blood flow","Destroy nephrons"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: renal prostaglandins vasodilate to preserve renal blood flow, particularly during states of RAAS or sympathetic activation — which is why NSAIDs (which block prostaglandin synthesis) can harm the kidney. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The kidneys receive approximately what percentage of cardiac output?","choices":["10%","5%","50%","22%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: despite their small mass, the kidneys receive roughly 20–25% of cardiac output. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Renal blood flow is determined by:","choices":["CO × BP","GFR × FF","Pressure gradient ÷ Resistance","Pressure × Resistance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: renal blood flow, like any flow, equals the pressure gradient divided by resistance (an application of Ohm's law to the renal circulation). — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which arteriole enters the glomerulus?","choices":["Arcuate","Efferent","Afferent","Interlobar"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the afferent arteriole carries blood into the glomerular capillary tuft. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"Which arteriole exits the glomerulus?","choices":["Afferent","Interlobular","Segmental","Efferent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the efferent arteriole drains blood out of the glomerulus toward the peritubular capillaries or vasa recta. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"The primary purpose of renal autoregulation is to maintain:","choices":["Constant sodium intake","Stable GFR despite changing blood pressure","Constant urine color","Constant plasma glucose"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: renal autoregulation (myogenic mechanism plus tubuloglomerular feedback) keeps GFR and renal blood flow relatively stable across a wide range of arterial pressures. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"A patient has nephrotic syndrome with damage to the filtration barrier. Which finding is MOST likely?","choices":["Albuminuria","Increased renin production","Glycosuria only","RBC destruction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: damage to the glomerular filtration barrier lets albumin leak through, producing the albuminuria/proteinuria that defines nephrotic syndrome. — Renal Physiology I lecture"},{"q":"A patient has severe dehydration. Renal perfusion decreases, NaCl delivery to the macula densa decreases, and renin is released. Which physiological response helps preserve GFR?","choices":["Increased Bowman's capsule pressure","Afferent dilation with no hormonal response","Increased Angiotensin II causing preferential efferent arteriolar constriction","Increased ANP release"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: angiotensin II preferentially constricts the efferent arteriole, backing up pressure within the glomerulus (raising PG) to help preserve GFR even as renal perfusion falls. — Renal Physiology I lecture"}]};
